## Migration Analysis

Your code has three thread-abort patterns that **will not work on .NET 8** (`Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`):

| Pattern Found | Location | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` + `Abort()` fallback | `CancellationToken` |
| **ThreadAbortException as control flow** | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` with rollback | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Preventing rethrow to keep thread alive | Remove — `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — OperationCanceledException doesn't auto-rethrow.
                // Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        // If still alive after Join, the thread is IsBackground=true
        // and will be torn down on process exit.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes & Pitfalls

**1. `GetNextItem` must accept the token.** If it blocks (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take`), pass the token so it wakes on cancel. Without this, cancellation stalls until the blocking call returns on its own:
```csharp
// Before: may block indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
var item = queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
var item = queue.Take(token);
```

**2. `Thread.Sleep` → `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne`.** If any code path uses `Thread.Sleep`, replace it — `Sleep` ignores the token:
```csharp
// Before
Thread.Sleep(1000);

// After — wakes immediately on cancellation
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000);
```

**3. Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It's `IDisposable` and leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed. The class now implements `IDisposable`.

**4. Cancellation is cooperative, not instant.** Unlike `Thread.Abort` which injected an exception at any point, cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add checks inside it:
```csharp
private void ProcessItem(WorkItem item, CancellationToken token)
{
    foreach (var step in item.Steps)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Execute(step);
    }
}
```

**5. No force-kill equivalent exists.** If the thread truly can't cooperate (e.g., calls unmanaged code that blocks forever), the only option is to move that work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. For your code, cooperative cancellation is sufficient.
